        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Sealing an asphalt driveway is worth it when the pavement is structurally sound, past its first year, and starting to gray. In that case a coat every two to three years plus prompt crack filling slows surface aging, keeps water out, and keeps the driveway looking maintained for a modest cost.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            It is not worth it on brand-new asphalt, on a driveway sealed recently that still sheds water, or on pavement with a failing base. Skip coal tar where it is restricted, apply thin coats on clean, dry pavement, and treat sealer as surface maintenance rather than a cure for structural problems.
          </p>
